{"version_id": 83, "article_id": 9, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Science Needs Fairer Rules to Survive Conflict", "title_zh": "战火之下，科学合作需要更公平的规则", "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03176-y", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49705390741_8c22b1fd8a_b.jpg", "credit": "USDAgov · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 249, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "Science has often been seen as a field that can rise above politics. However, growing global tensions are now changing how researchers work together, and some scientists say the rules must be fairer.", "lead_zh": "科学常被视为能超越政治的领域。然而，日益紧张的国际局势正在改变科研合作的方式，一些科学家认为规则必须变得更公平。", "preview": "For a long time, scientific collaboration was one of the few areas that could go beyond geopolitics. Researchers from different countries wo", "preview_zh": "长期以来，科学合作是少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。来自不同国家的研究人员携手工作，尽管他们的政府有时意见不合。然而，随着全球紧张局势加剧，一个意想不到的问题出现了。如今，科学家所在的机构正在变成一种政治标签。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 73, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@6f046c1/content/audio/9/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 104.513, "size_bytes": 630864, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 281, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@6f046c1/content/data/versions/83.json", "paragraphs": ["For a long time, scientific collaboration was one of the few areas that could go beyond geopolitics. Researchers from different countries worked together, although their governments sometimes disagreed. However, as global tensions grow, an unexpected problem has appeared. Now, the institution a scientist works for is becoming a kind of political label.", "This issue was raised in a letter published in Nature on 06 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh from Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi from Zhejiang Normal University, both in China. They argue that fairer rules are needed so that science can keep moving forward during conflict.", "The writers do not say that politics should be ignored, because that would not be realistic. Instead, they suggest that researchers should be judged by their work, not by the country or university they belong to. If institutions become political symbols, then many talented scientists may lose chances to join important projects.", "Related articles in Nature also discuss this problem. One piece looks at academic boycotts, and another explains how geopolitical tensions are making China rethink its research partnerships. Together, these reports show that the problem is not small, and it affects scientists in many parts of the world.", "Science works best when ideas and people can move freely across borders. Although conflicts will not disappear soon, the authors believe that clearer and fairer rules can protect research. In this way, scientists can continue to solve global problems together, even when their governments do not agree."], "paragraphs_zh": ["长期以来，科学合作是少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。来自不同国家的研究人员携手工作，尽管他们的政府有时意见不合。然而，随着全球紧张局势加剧，一个意想不到的问题出现了。如今，科学家所在的机构正在变成一种政治标签。", "这个问题在2026年10月6日发表于《自然》杂志的一封信中被提出。作者是上海大学的Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh和浙江师范大学的Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi，两人都在中国。他们认为，需要更公平的规则，才能让科学在冲突中继续前进。", "作者并没有说应该无视政治，因为那并不现实。相反，他们建议评判研究人员应看其工作成果，而不是看他们来自哪个国家或哪所大学。如果机构变成政治符号，许多有才华的科学家可能会失去参与重要项目的机会。", "《自然》杂志的相关文章也讨论了这个问题。一篇关注学术抵制，另一篇解释地缘政治紧张如何促使中国重新思考科研合作。这些报道共同表明，问题并不小，它影响着世界许多地方的科学家。", "当思想和人员能够自由跨越国界时，科学才能发挥最大作用。尽管冲突不会很快消失，作者相信更清晰、更公平的规则可以保护科研。这样，即使政府之间意见不合，科学家们仍能继续共同解决全球性问题。"], "body": "For a long time, scientific collaboration was one of the few areas that could go beyond geopolitics. Researchers from different countries worked together, although their governments sometimes disagreed. However, as global tensions grow, an unexpected problem has appeared. Now, the institution a scientist works for is becoming a kind of political label.\n\nThis issue was raised in a letter published in Nature on 06 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh from Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi from Zhejiang Normal University, both in China. They argue that fairer rules are needed so that science can keep moving forward during conflict.\n\nThe writers do not say that politics should be ignored, because that would not be realistic. Instead, they suggest that researchers should be judged by their work, not by the country or university they belong to. If institutions become political symbols, then many talented scientists may lose chances to join important projects.\n\nRelated articles in Nature also discuss this problem. One piece looks at academic boycotts, and another explains how geopolitical tensions are making China rethink its research partnerships. Together, these reports show that the problem is not small, and it affects scientists in many parts of the world.\n\nScience works best when ideas and people can move freely across borders. Although conflicts will not disappear soon, the authors believe that clearer and fairer rules can protect research. In this way, scientists can continue to solve global problems together, even when their governments do not agree.", "body_zh": "长期以来，科学合作是少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。来自不同国家的研究人员携手工作，尽管他们的政府有时意见不合。然而，随着全球紧张局势加剧，一个意想不到的问题出现了。如今，科学家所在的机构正在变成一种政治标签。\n\n这个问题在2026年10月6日发表于《自然》杂志的一封信中被提出。作者是上海大学的Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh和浙江师范大学的Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi，两人都在中国。他们认为，需要更公平的规则，才能让科学在冲突中继续前进。\n\n作者并没有说应该无视政治，因为那并不现实。相反，他们建议评判研究人员应看其工作成果，而不是看他们来自哪个国家或哪所大学。如果机构变成政治符号，许多有才华的科学家可能会失去参与重要项目的机会。\n\n《自然》杂志的相关文章也讨论了这个问题。一篇关注学术抵制，另一篇解释地缘政治紧张如何促使中国重新思考科研合作。这些报道共同表明，问题并不小，它影响着世界许多地方的科学家。\n\n当思想和人员能够自由跨越国界时，科学才能发挥最大作用。尽管冲突不会很快消失，作者相信更清晰、更公平的规则可以保护科研。这样，即使政府之间意见不合，科学家们仍能继续共同解决全球性问题。", "text": "For a long time, scientific collaboration was one of the few areas that could go beyond geopolitics. Researchers from different countries worked together, although their governments sometimes disagreed. However, as global tensions grow, an unexpected problem has appeared. Now, the institution a scientist works for is becoming a kind of political label. This issue was raised in a letter published in Nature on 06 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh from Shanghai University and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi from Zhejiang Normal University, both in China. They argue that fairer rules are needed so that science can keep moving forward during conflict. The writers do not say that politics should be ignored, because that would not be realistic. Instead, they suggest that researchers should be judged by their work, not by the country or university they belong to. If institutions become political symbols, then many talented scientists may lose chances to join important projects. Related articles in Nature also discuss this problem. One piece looks at academic boycotts, and another explains how geopolitical tensions are making China rethink its research partnerships. Together, these reports show that the problem is not small, and it affects scientists in many parts of the world. Science works best when ideas and people can move freely across borders. Although conflicts will not disappear soon, the authors believe that clearer and fairer rules can protect research. In this way, scientists can continue to solve global problems together, even when their governments do not agree.", "vocab": [{"word": "collaboration", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合作", "note": "常与 between/with 连用，指多方一起工作"}, {"word": "geopolitics", "pos": "noun", "zh": "地缘政治", "note": "指国家间因地理位置和权力产生的政治关系"}, {"word": "tension", "pos": "noun", "zh": "紧张局势", "note": "常用复数 tensions，指国家或人群间的紧张关系"}, {"word": "institution", "pos": "noun", "zh": "机构；大学", "note": "这里指大学、研究所等科研单位"}, {"word": "affiliation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "隶属关系；所属机构", "note": "常指一个人所属的组织或单位"}, {"word": "proxy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "代表；替代物", "note": "a proxy for 表示“某事物的代表或象征”"}, {"word": "conflict", "pos": "noun", "zh": "冲突", "note": "可指战争，也可指意见或利益的对立"}, {"word": "boycott", "pos": "noun", "zh": "抵制", "note": "指拒绝购买、参加或合作以示抗议"}, {"word": "partnership", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合作关系", "note": "常与 research 搭配，research partnership 即科研合作"}, {"word": "realistic", "pos": "adj", "zh": "现实的；实际的", "note": "形容想法符合实际情况"}], "title_original": "Maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 06 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "body_original": "CORRESPONDENCE\n06 October 2026\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules\nBy\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\n0\n&\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\n1\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\nShanghai University, Shanghai, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\nZhejiang Normal University, Jinhua, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nScientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity.\nAccess options\nAccess through your institution\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\n$32.99\n/ 30 days\ncancel any time\nLearn more\nSubscribe to this journal\nReceive 52 print issues and online access\n$199.00 per year\nonly $3.83 per issue\nLearn more\nRent or buy this article\nPrices vary by article type\nfrom\n$1.95\nto\n$39.95\nLearn more\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkou